+## Default credentials
+
+| Service | User  | Password |
+|---------|-------|----------|
+| SSH     | root  | admin    |
+| Web UI  | admin | admin    |
+
+Change these on first use: SSH and the web UI are both open on the WAN by
+default. Change the web password under *Settings → Change admin password*, and
+root's password with `passwd` over SSH. The web password is stored hashed
+(pbkdf2) in `/etc/rnsbox/auth.json`. There is one session at a time: its
+token is random per login and kept only in RAM (`/run`), and a new login ends
+any other. Every state-changing portal action is a POST that must come from
+the portal's own page (same-origin `Origin`/`Referer` check); a plain link or
+a cross-site form can't change anything.
+
+Open on the WAN by default (*Network → Open ports*): `tcp 22` (SSH),
+`tcp 80` (the web UI, plus `8081` for the modem page) and `tcp 4242`
+(`rnsd`'s `TCPServerInterface`, for Reticulum peers). Remove any of them
+there to close it. The LAN side (the USB-C link, plus the WiFi hotspot while
+it is on) always has full access.
+
+The WiFi **hotspot has no default password**: it stays off until you set one
+(8–63 characters) on the WiFi tab. The old published default `changeme123` is
+refused, and a box still carrying it keeps its hotspot off (the WiFi client
+side keeps working) until a new password is set.
 
 ## WiFi on the Nano-e W
 
 The **W** board adds a single AIC8800 radio, driven from the portal's **WiFi**
 tab. It has four modes: **off**, **STA** (join an upstream network), **AP** (run
-a hotspot), and **STA + AP** (both at once). On the plain Nano-e, which has no
-radio, WiFi bring-up is a clean no-op.
+a hotspot), and **STA + AP** (both at once). The hotspot runs on 2.4 GHz,
+and since the radio can't be on two bands at once, STA + AP pins the WiFi
+client to 2.4 GHz too: an upstream that only offers 5 GHz won't connect in
+that mode, so use STA-only for a 5 GHz uplink. The WiFi client can be the WAN
+(*Network → WAN uplink → wlan0*; always DHCP). The hotspot (`RNSBox-AP`,
+`192.168.42.1/24`) is a second LAN: its clients get DHCP, DNS, the portal and
+NAT'd internet, like the USB-C LAN.
 
 Because the AIC8800 is a **single radio**, the STA and AP time-share it, and the
 hotspot is treated as the priority so local clients stay served:
