+### Running without a trusted clock
+
+Reticulum still routes without a trusted clock, but its announces carry a
+wrong (1970) timestamp, which has side effects:
+
+- **Stale paths.** Reticulum keeps a path until the current time passes its
+  receive time plus 7 days, and replaces it only with an announce emitted
+  later than the stored one. So a node whose clock is behind the timestamps
+  it stored keeps stale paths indefinitely. This box gets there after a
+  reboot when it ran with NTP or a browser-set clock, or for days on an unset
+  one. `S82rnsd` therefore sets aside a path table dated more than an hour
+  after the current clock before it starts `rnsd` (identity and known
+  destinations are kept), and the box re-learns its paths from live
+  announces. On an online box without an RTC that is usually one re-learn per
+  boot, since `rnsd` starts before NTP sets the clock.
+- **Peers.** Peers that still hold this box's earlier, later-dated announce
+  ignore its new ones until that path expires on their own clock. Another
+  RNSBox whose clock also rolled back sets its table aside at its own
+  restart; any other node whose clock rolled back keeps the stale path until
+  its clock is set.
+- **When the clock is set** later, Reticulum re-learns paths and
+  re-establishes links once, and timestamps are correct from then on.
+- **Propagation nodes** (`lxmd`, or `nomadnet`'s) record with the unset date
+  too: the messages they store, the propagation peers they hear, and their
+  record of messages already handled. When the clock is set, RNSBox's LXMF
+  patch re-dates all of these rather than expiring them, so messages still
+  get LXMF's full 30-day lifetime, peers heard meanwhile aren't dropped as
+  14 days unreachable, and already-handled messages aren't accepted again.
+- **The rnsd update check** needs a correct date for HTTPS, so it is skipped
+  (and the portal says so) until the clock is set.
+
+A DS3231, or NTP or a browser sync on every node, avoids all of this.
 
