+## Reticulum services
+
+- **`rnsd`** reads `/etc/reticulum/config`, which the Reticulum tab can edit
+  (*Save*, then *Restart rnsd*); the same tab shows `rnstatus -a`. Its
+  transport identity lives under `/etc/reticulum/storage`, readable by root
+  only. If `rnsd` crashes, `S46wanwatch` starts it again within about 10 s;
+  an explicit `/etc/init.d/S82rnsd stop` sticks until the next `start` or
+  `restart`.
+- **Auto-restart** (optional, off by default): Reticulum tab → *Auto-restart
+  rnsd*, every 1–7 days at 04:00. `rnsd`'s RAM grows as it learns the network
+  (an announce cache, not a leak); a periodic restart bounds it.
+- **Updates.** The box checks PyPI for a newer `rns` daily at 05:00 and once
+  per boot (once the clock is set and a default route exists), and shows a
+  notice on the Reticulum tab; the automatic checks (daily and at boot) can
+  be switched off there, and *Check for updates* runs one on demand. It
+  never installs anything by itself. *Update rnsd … now* replaces the `rns`
+  package in place with `pip` and restarts `rnsd`; your config is untouched,
+  and it rolls back if `rnsd` fails to come back up.
+- **NomadNet and `lxmd`** ship installed but disabled (`enabled=no`): the
+  256 MB board can't comfortably run them beside `rnsd` for long. To enable
+  one, set `enabled=yes` in `/etc/rnsbox/nomadnet.conf` or
+  `/etc/rnsbox/lxmd.conf`, then reboot or run `/etc/init.d/S83nomadnet start`
+  / `/etc/init.d/S85lxmd start`. Both attach to `rnsd`'s shared instance
+  instead of starting a second stack. `lxmd` refuses to start when less than
+  `min_free_mb` (60 MB by default) of memory is available. Stopping either
+  one also ends the stamp workers LXMF forks, so none are left running. Run
+  one propagation node, not both: `lxmd` is the lightweight choice when that
+  is all you want; NomadNet adds a pages server.
+
+## Logs
+
+All logs are in RAM (`/var/log` lives on the 16 MB `/tmp` tmpfs), so they
+don't wear the microSD card and start empty at every boot.
+
+- `/var/log/messages` — syslog, rotated. `rnsd`, `lxmd` and `nomadnet` log
+  here (tags `rnsd[pid]` etc.).
+- `/var/log/ntp-step.log` — the NTP one-shot and the hourly RTC resync.
+- `/var/log/wanwatch.log` — the background watchdog (NTP retries, `rnsd`
+  restarts, eth0 re-plugs).
+- `/var/log/wifi.log` — WiFi bring-up and the STA + AP watchdog.
+- `/var/log/slip.log` — the SLIP link to the HaLow modem.
+- `/var/log/nftgen.err` — firewall generation problems.
+- `/var/log/cron.log` — scheduled jobs (auto-restart, update check, RTC
+  resync).
+- `/var/log/resizefs.log` — growing the rootfs to fill the card at boot
+  (`resize2fs`).
+
+eth0's DHCP client (`udhcpc`) is not logged, on purpose: with no DHCP server
+answering it would flood syslog. eth0 re-plugs show in `wanwatch.log`.
+
+## Build variants
+
+```
+./fetch-clients.sh        # dvd only: download the client apps first
+./build-rnsbox.sh dvd     # full image + read-only clients disc (~3.3 GB now)
+./build-rnsbox.sh lite    # NCM-only image, no clients disc (~226 MB)
+```
+
+The DVD image is sized to fit the client disc, so its size follows the
+current client releases. See [APPLYING.md](APPLYING.md) for the full flow.
+
+## Known follow-ups
+
+- `fetch-clients.sh` (run before a DVD build) downloads the **latest**
+  release of each client app; nothing is version-pinned. It checks each
+  download against the checksum files a project publishes, but where a
+  project publishes none, or its checksum file fails to download, it only
+  records the file's SHA-256 in `dl/clients/MANIFEST.txt`
+  (shipped on the disc as `SHA256SUMS.txt`). So the disc's contents depend on
+  what upstream published when they were fetched. It also keeps earlier
+  downloads, and the DVD build puts every installer under `dl/clients/` on
+  the disc, so delete `dl/clients/` before fetching newer releases. Treat the
+  clients disc as optional and review `dl/clients/` and
+  `build/clients-stick/` before relying on it. The `lite` build does not need
+  it.
